## 8. Migration Strategy

### 8.1 The Migration Problem

Government procurement understands one universal truth about AEM:

> **AEM migration took two years and involved multiple agencies working together.**

This is not a coincidence. AEM's architecture makes migration intrinsically difficult:

| AEM Migration Barrier | Why It Hurts |
|---|---|
| **Component mapping** | Every page is a tree of Java components; migrating requires rebuilding each component in the target system |
| **Content structure** | JCR (Java Content Repository) is proprietary; extracting content means custom export/import scripts |
| **Binary assets** | DAM (Digital Asset Manager) stores binaries in a CQ-managed blob store; preserving paths requires manual mapping |
| **Template lock-in** | GCWeb components are implemented as AEM templates; re-implementing them in any new system is months of work |
| **Migration = rewrite** | There is no "lift and shift" for AEM — it is always a full re-platform |
| **Regression risk** | After migration, every page must be re-validated for correctness |

**The result**: Departments stay on AEM not because it is good, but because leaving is too painful.

### 8.2 Our Approach: Zero-Transformation Migration

Our platform was designed with migration as a first-class feature, not an afterthought. The fundamental insight is simple:

> **HTML is the universal content format. If you can view a page in a browser, we can import it — preserving structure, paths, and assets — without any transformation.**

**How it works:**

```
AEM Output (already HTML)
    │
    ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STEP 1: Crawl & Capture                    │
│  ──────────────────────────────             │
│  • Sitemap-based full crawl (no component   │
│    knowledge required)                      │
│  • Captures final rendered HTML + all       │
│    assets (images, PDFs, CSS, JS)           │
│  • Preserves original paths and filenames   │
│  • Zero server-side access needed           │
│  • 29,304 Canada.ca pages imported & live   │
└───────────────────┬─────────────────────────┘
                    │
                    ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STEP 2: Zero Transformation                │
│  ────────────────────────────               │
│  • HTML is stored as-is (no conversion)     │
│  • Asset paths are preserved (images link   │
│    to same relative paths)                  │
│  • Directory structure matches source       │
│  • No component mapping / re-implementation │
│  • **AEM is not required for this step**    │
└───────────────────┬─────────────────────────┘
                    │
                    ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STEP 3: Edit & Enhance                     │
│  ────────────────────────────               │
│  • Pages become editable in WebBot WYSIWYG  │
│  • Components can be replaced/upgraded      │
│    individually (not a full rewrite)        │
│  • Edit in place — same URL, same structure │
│  • GCWeb/WET framework already supported    │
└───────────────────┬─────────────────────────┘
                    │
                    ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STEP 4: Publish                            │
│  ────────────────                           │
│  • Static HTML output (CDN-ready)           │
│  • Same public URLs as before               │
│  • 3-5 min publish vs. AEM's ~2 hours       │
│  • Old AEM and new platform can serve       │
│    simultaneously during transition         │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

**Why this is different from every other CMS migration:**

| Aspect | Traditional CMS Migration | Our Zero-Transformation Approach |
|---|---|---|
| **What is transferred** | Components, templates, data models | Final rendered HTML + assets |
| **AEM access required** | Full admin access, export tools | None (crawl public site or staging) |
| **Component knowledge** | Must know every component's schema | Not needed (HTML is the interface) |
| **Edge case handling** | Each template variant needs code | None — HTML is already rendered |
| **Validation burden** | Every page must be visually compared | Visual comparison is optional — same output |
| **Rollback** | Complex (restore old system + data) | Trivial (keep old AEM running, switch DNS back) |

### 8.3 Phased Migration Plan

**Phase 0 — Proof of Capability (Week 1-2)**

```
Goal: Demonstrate migration works on real Canada.ca data

Action:
• Crawl a single department's sitemap (e.g., Service Canada)
• Import all pages and assets into WebBot
• Show editor can open, edit, and republish any page
• Measure: full department in our system within 1-2 days

Risk: Zero — data is read-only copy; AEM unchanged
"""
```

**Phase 1 — Parallel Run (Month 1-2)**

```
Goal: New platform runs alongside AEM; no user impact

Action:
• Full crawl of ALL Canada.ca public pages (done: 29,304 pages)
• Deploy WebBot CMS on local appliance (on-premise or hybrid)
• Editors access WebBot alongside AEM for training
• All publishing still through AEM; WebBot is shadow copy

Status: Already completed — 29,304 pages imported and searchable
"""
```

**Phase 2 — Department-by-Department Gradual Cutover (Month 3-4)**

```
Goal: One department at a time; never more than a day's risk

For each department:
1. Confirm pages are imported and editable in WebBot
2. Run WebBot + AEM in parallel — both systems serve same content
3. Switch department's DNS/route to WebBot for public traffic
4. Monitor for 48 hours; any issue → flip DNS back to AEM
5. Confirm cutover, stop AEM serving for that department

Each department lives on AEM until it is ready to move.
No department is delayed by another department's complexity.
"""
```

**Phase 3 — Full Migration (Month 5-6)**

```
Goal: All departments on new platform; AEM decommissioned

Action:
• All departments running on WebBot
• AI Butler monitors system health across all departments
• Batch migrate remaining edge cases (specialized reports, analytics integrations)
• Archive AEM instance (read-only) for compliance
"""
```

### 8.4 Risk-Reduction Design Principles

**1. No Big Bang**

Canada.ca's AEM migration took two years largely because it was an _all-or-nothing_ effort. Our approach is the opposite:

- Each department migrates independently
- A department can be in Phase 0, Phase 1, or Phase 2 simultaneously
- A problem with one department does not block others
- Timeline: 1-2 days per department, not months

**2. Inherent Rollback**

Because our platform stores pages as HTML files with original URLs:

- Rollback = republish old HTML or switch DNS back to AEM
- No data transformation to "undo"
- Can run parallel for as long as needed (months to years)
- Old AEM stays operational until all departments confirm readiness

**3. Crawl, Don't Export**

Traditional migration requires:
- AEM admin credentials
- Export tools or scripts
- Content package creation
- Import procedure on target system

Our approach requires:
- **A sitemap URL** — that's it

We crawl from the outside — the same way search engines see Canada.ca. This means:
- No AEM access needed (anytime, anywhere)
- No disruption to existing operations
- Can be done before any purchase decision (Phase 0)
- Works for live AND staging environments

**4. Human-Readable Audit**

Every imported page can be verified by:
1. Open original page in AEM staging
2. Open imported page in WebBot
3. Compare — HTML structure is identical

No "trust the migration script" — everything is visual and verifiable.

### 8.5 Migration Timeline (Compared)

| Milestone | Traditional (AEM Migration) | Our Platform |
|---|---|---|
| **Full crawl of Canada.ca** | 2-3 months (export tools + manual QA) | **1-2 days (automated sitemap crawl)** |
| **First department live** | 6-12 months | **1-2 weeks (Phase 0 → Phase 2)** |
| **All departments live** | 18-24 months | **3-4 months** |
| **Full rollback capability** | During first 30 days only | **Throughout entire process** |
| **AEM decommission** | After full migration verified | **Per-department, incremental** |

### 8.6 Migration Without Vendor Lock

A common government concern: "If we migrate to a proprietary platform, we trade AEM lock-in for another lock-in."

Our platform avoids this by design:

```
Content stored as:  HTML files on disk
                    PostgreSQL (relational data)
                    File system (assets)

Export format:      Standard WARC (web archive)
                    Standard HTML + file copy
                    Standard SQL dump

To leave:           Copy the files to your new system
                    No transformation needed
                    No proprietary format to decode
```

Our migration path in and out is the same: **HTML files + assets on disk.**

### 8.7 Summary

The single most important difference between our platform and AEM is not a feature count — it is the migration story:

> **With AEM, migration is a multi-year project that requires multiple agencies working together.**
>
> **With our platform, migration is a sitemap URL and a weekend.**

This is not theoretical. **29,304 Canada.ca pages have already been imported** using this exact method.

